  "contributors": "Christopher Daniel Pang supplied the Dyson lead, one complete turn per doubling, the retained-frame question and the two orientations. Hearthline derived the octave aperture and the stated prime-side inequalities in his constructive program.",
  "formal_scope": "Two-observer energy, actual model with off-line shift log(9/8)/log(2), two one-turn observation integrals, critical positivity, no off-axis erasure, explicit contribution at square 4, and the analytic centered-response implication to stock RH.",
  "paper_scope": "Both one-sided square tests for the full prime-power tent and the primes-only upper inequality are equivalent to RH. The missing proof is the global upper inequality for the actual primes. A case split proves unconditional infinitely recurring deficits and surpluses on actual ordinary square heads.",
  "self_review": {
    "claim": "For every integer j>=2, the primes-only triangular reading at j^2 is strictly below c*j if and only if RH is true, with the universal quantifier outside the inequality.",
    "primary_verdict": "proved as written (the equivalence, not the universal inequality)",
    "type": "author self-review",
    "audited_reader_sha256": "4a94c4a4d496bdf480ee70cd84a9fd906aad86c9789f6cb932a3705d03d0cd94",
    "audited_derivation_sha256": "3d477271e02e158227e8758ac080fd41ac023577acf1d9cd554e283007b8c5a7",
    "obligations": {
      "weight_orientation": "passed: on a prime square the von Mangoldt value is log(p), not twice log(p). Endpoints carry zero; n=1 contributes nothing.",
      "quadratic_control": "passed: the double-centered and split models have identical height multisets; their raw response differs by 1/36. The shortfall at square 4 is a separate true arithmetic calculation.",
      "nonvanishing_and_multiple_roots": "passed: the octave multiplier is nonzero for positive real displacement. The logarithmic derivative detects every positive finite order, including multiple zeros.",
      "real_abscissa": "passed on paper: the pole at s=1/2 cancels with exactly K(1/2). A one-sided sampled bound interpolates to O(log x) and the nonnegative Mellin argument applies. The holomorphic extension implies stock RH independently in Lean.",
      "actual_diagonal": "passed: support [1/2,2], the first prime occurs only at an endpoint, and the real-place integral has its removable endpoint value. Outward rectangular bounds use the separate monotonicities of both positive factors.",
      "prime_only_return": "passed: square mass is uniformly bounded without RH, so a prime-only upper bound suffices. Under RH the square mass is larger than the actual spectral ceiling for j>=32768; at earlier roots apart from 4 and 9 the finite bound suffices. Those two cases are computed directly.",
      "recurrence": "passed on paper: positive summable critical weights admit a simultaneous recurrence on dyadic integers under RH. Failure of RH precludes either finite eventual one-sided bound. No independence of ordinates or external RH proof is used."
    },
    "global_prime_bound": "open; lower check after removing powers and unrestricted change of aperture both have explicit counterexamples"
  },
